  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between 13-cis-Retinol and Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, mediated through a series of targets. 13-cis-Retinol is directly linked to Potassium-transporting ATPase subunit beta, which in turn is a target of multiple compounds, including Labradorin 1 and Anhydrofurospongin-1. These compounds are all implicated in the regulation of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, the enzyme responsible for a critical step in glycolysis. The consistent scores across the three paths, all with a final score of 0.5733, indicate a strong level of confidence in the proposed relationship. The evidence suggests that 13-cis-Retinol may influence the activity of Potassium-transporting ATPase subunit beta, which then affects the activity of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through its downstream targets. This mechanism highlights a potential pathway by which 13-cis-Retinol could exert its biological effects on metabolic processes.",
